      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let the two numbers be $x$ and $y$, where $x > y$. We are given two equations:\n\n$x + y = 6 \\quad \\text{(1)}$\n\n$x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\quad \\text{(2)}$\n\nWe can solve equation (1) for $y$:\n\n$y = 6 - x \\quad \\text{(3)}$\n\nNow, substitute equation (3) into equation (2):\n\n$x^2 - (6 - x)^2 = 12$\n\nExpand and simplify:\n\n$x^2 - (36 - 12x + x^2) = 12$\n\n$-36 + 12x = 12$\n\n$LHS = -36 + 12x - 12x = -36$\n\n$-36 = -12$\n\nWe can cancel the negative sign on both sides:\n\n$36 = 12$\n\n$36 / 12 = 12 / 12$\n\n$3 = 1$\n\nSince $3 \\neq 1$, there is no positive difference for $x$ and $y$\n\n\nAlternative solution without the square root:\n\nWe have $x^2 - y^2 = 12$ and $x + y = 6$, which means (given the interval) $x$ and $y$ are different from 3. We can then write $ (x - y)^2 = (x + y)^2 - 4xy = 12 - 4xy = 12$. Hence,$ x^2 + y^2 = 24 $. Continuing on from $x^2 + (6 - x)^2 = 24 $ decomposes to a quadratic for the quadratic. From there, since they asked for the positive difference, it is now $$ \\boxed{2} $$",
